The Story of the Little Squirrel and the Owl’s Pit
Once upon a time, deep in a serene forest, lived a little squirrel named Nosy. Nosy wasn't your ordinary squirrel. Instead of spending his days gathering acorns and storing them up for the cold winter ahead like the others, he spent every waking hour scurrying around the woods, spying on what everyone else was doing.

On Monday, he lurked behind a bush to watch the bear fish, only to chatter loudly across the river: "You’re doing it all wrong! That technique will never catch a trout!"

On Tuesday, he watched a trail of ants carrying food back to their hill and interrupted: "Why take this path? Where’s the optimization? This anthill has terrible logistics!"

The other animals just sighed in exhaustion. Nobody had ever asked for Nosy’s opinion, yet he operated on a strict personal motto: "Other people’s business is my primary duty."

Then came Wednesday. Nosy spotted the old, wise owl working feverishly at the base of a massive oak tree, digging a mysterious secret pit. The owl dug quietly, methodically, without uttering a single word to anyone passing by.

Instantly, Nosy’s eyes sparkled. "Aha! That old owl must be hiding a secret treasure! Or maybe he’s up to no good? I must intervene! I must fix it! I have to know!"

Without a second thought, Nosy ignored his own nest—which had developed a noticeable leak—and forgot all about foraging for his evening meal. He crept closer and closer until he plunged his head right into the opening of the pit, shouting at the top of his lungs:

"Hey, hold on there! You’re digging that all wrong, old owl! It needs to be much deeper! And why do it alone in secret? Want me to jump in and rewrite your plans for you?!"

The owl paused, lifted his huge, round eyes, and let out a long, heavy sigh. In a low, steady voice, he replied:

"Little squirrel... I am not digging for hidden treasure, nor am I orchestrating anything grand. I am simply digging a hole to bury the odorous waste I collected to clean up this part of the forest. And the reason I kept quiet is because it brings zero value to anyone else's life."

Before Nosy could even process the words, the owl dumped a massive load of foul-smelling compost straight into the pit. A thick, overwhelming cloud of stench billowed up, hitting Nosy right in the face. He sputtered, coughed, choked, and staggered backward with watery eyes.

The owl looked down at the wheezing squirrel and offered one final thought:

"People often love to barge in, pry open, inspect, and tamper with things when the owner never asked for their help. Sometimes, someone is just trying to deal with their own trash. When you jump into affairs that aren't yours, you won't always find gold—more often than not, you just end up covered in someone else's mess and baggage."

The owl gestured toward the trees above. "Now, if I were you, I'd go check on your own nest. It looks like it's about to collapse in the rain."

Shocked and mortified, Nosy scrambled as fast as he could back to his tree branch, only to find that other forest critters had carried off his remaining food supply—all because he was so busy managing everyone else's business that he completely neglected his own!

Moral of the story: Before you sneak in to edit someone else's files or pry into their business, make sure your own "nest" is fully sorted out first!
